A1. A politician's buy-word. (plur.) ~ GRAFTS
A7. Rapid travelers of the air. ~ FLASHES
A14. A man usually does this after he winds the clock. ~ RETIRES
A16. This describes a shiny nose. ~ RADIANT
A17. This is a small proviso. ~ IF
A18. What a cheerful liver gives. ~ BILE
A19. A press agent. (This is a hot one.) ~ IRON
A20. Toward. ~ TO
A21. Save this for a rainy day. ~ MUD
A23. Established. ~ PLACED
A25. Had knowledge. (If the back-seat driver doesn't decide other wise.) ~ KNEW
A27. This is a very small matter ~ ATOM
A29. Only two letters for the editor to-day. ~ ED
A30. This is bad for the digestion. ~ IRE
A31. What a good wife does for her husband. ~ CARES
A33. This comes high in Chicago. ~ EL
A34. What they did when "the gang was all there." (What the heck did they care?) ~ HAILED
A37. What the circus used to do to the small boy. ~ ELATE
A38. Cuckoo! ~ DEMENTED
A40. This is a tight spot. ~ EDINBURGH
A42. At night these silently steal away. ~ BURGLARS
A44. What the professor had when he tried the bed-post to see if it was in neutral. ~ LAPSE
A48. This one is a hanger-on. ~ TASSEL
A49. The western half of Asia. ~ AS
A50. What Mr. Gann missed in Washington. ~ ECLAT
A51. This one is in favor. ~ AYE
A52. Near. ~ AT
A53. A gas consumer. ~ AUTO
A54. Figuratively speaking this is a matter of form. ~ BODY
A56. What cigarettes will do to women. ~ PLEASE
A60. This is good for the whoopee cough. (Hie-hie.) ~ GIN
A61. The way the neighbors keep their radio. ~ ON
A62. What intelligent people do to "Judge" every week. ~ EDIT
A64. Bolsheviki. ~ REDS
A66. A lazy sluggish fellow with only 3 toes and one i. ~ AI
A67. What the ambitious young man may do when he seeks a new position. ~ RECLINE
A69. A brilliant crimson aniline dye. ~ MAGENTA
A71. A good thing to put in the tub before taking a bath. ~ STOPPER
A72. Pertaining to fast time in Chicago. ~ LENTEN

D1. A facial make-up. ~ GRIMACE
D2. A disproof. ~ REFUTAL
D3. Same as No. 52 Horizontal. ~ AT
D4. What a woman will do when she lies with her eyes closed. ~ FIB
D5. This is a good thing to take in your automobile. ~ TRIP
D6. This is what the ads on the road do. ~ SELL
D7. The kind of a chicken a butter and egg man usually takes in a night club. ~ FRIED
D8. If you want to get fat, try this. ~ LARD
D9. Here is some trouble for you. ~ ADO
D10. The only thing they didn't pack, before taking a trip. ~ SINK
D11. This will have to be repeated several times to make some people laugh. ~ HA
D12. This is what Dad did on his first day in college. ~ ENTERED
D13. Sh! This was done in dark cellars long, long ago. ~ STOWED
D15. This plays around the beaches and made quite a splash at Miami last winter. ~ SEA
D22. Beautiful but dumb. ~ DORA
D24. This is what they do when they make whoopee. ~ CELEBRATE
D26. A Green River. ~ NILE
D28. These are always going around in taxis. ~ METERS
D32. A grasslike herb. ~ SEDGE
D33. This one wrote "The Show Boat." Her last name begins with F. ~ EDNA
D34. This denotes a female is in possession. ~ HER
D35. What some people will do all day to lure some poor fish. ~ ANGLE
D36. A collegiate town in New York State. ~ ITHACA
D39. If you do this you are apt to make wrinkles. ~ MUSS
D41. This is what a boy who does not like to go to school, becomes. ~ ILL
D42. You may get stuck with this one. ~ BAYONET
D43. This is the kind of a car you will have next year. ~ USED
D45. This will put a stop to almost anything. ~ PLUG
D46. To surfeit. ~ SATIATE
D47. This one likes spats with Oxfords. (Quite English, don't you know!) ~ ETONIAN
D48. This is small and not hard to beat. (plur.) ~ TABORS
D52. You might do this if you want some change. ~ ALTER
D55. Producers who try to put on the dog will probably hear this about them. ~ YELP
D56. This is where they get the pitch in open air concerts. ~ PINE
D57. This is what Al Capone seems to have up his sleeve. ~ ARM
D58. The kind of a skin a woman likes. ~ SEAL
D59. This is the limit. ~ EDGE
D63. A good thing to take before breakfast. (And you don't have to go out 12 miles to get it.) ~ DIP
D65. A few like this could make quite a little change in Japan. ~ SEN
D68. These two characters make a company of their own. ~ CO
D70. No tresspassing. (abbr.) ~ NT


